# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum Channel {
  static var maxBytes: Int { get }
  static var maxHandles: Int { get }
  static var readSlot: Int { get }

  static func create() throws(Status) -> Ends

  struct Message: Sendable {
    var bytes: [UInt8]
    var handles: [UInt32]
    init(bytes: [UInt8], handles: [UInt32])
  }

  static func write(_ channel: borrowing Handle, bytes: [UInt8],
                    handles: [UInt32] = []) throws(Status)
  static func read(_ channel: borrowing Handle) throws(Status) -> Message

  struct Outgoing: Sendable {
    var channel: UInt32
    var bytes: [UInt8]
    var handles: [UInt32]
    var status: Status = .ok
    init(channel: UInt32, bytes: [UInt8], handles: [UInt32] = [])
  }

  struct Incoming: Sendable {
    var channel: UInt32
    var message: Message?
    var status: Status = .ok
    init(channel: UInt32)
  }

  @discardableResult
  static func write(_ items: inout [Outgoing]) throws(Status) -> Int
  @discardableResult
  static func read(_ items: inout [Incoming]) throws(Status) -> Int

  static func call(_ channel: borrowing Handle, bytes: [UInt8],
                   handles: [UInt32] = [],
                   deadline: Int64 = infiniteDeadline) throws(Status)
    -> Message
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A channel is a pair of ends; a message written to one end is read from the other.
A message is bytes and handles.
`create` returns the two ends as an `Ends`.
A message holds at most `maxBytes` bytes (65536) and `maxHandles` handles (64).

A `Message` is what a read returns.
The handles in it are raw numbers, now in this process's table and this process's to close or to wrap in `Handle(raw:)`; see sys-handle(2).

`write(_:bytes:handles:)` queues a message on the peer of `channel`.
The handles listed are moved into the message, and are consumed even if the write fails.
`read(_:)` returns the next message, or throws `shouldWait` if none is queued, and `peerClosed` if none is queued and the other end is gone.
A message of any size is read whole.
To wait for one, bind the channel's readable signal to a port, or use `Handle.wait`.
They are `channel_write(2)` and `channel_read(2)`.

## Batches

`write(_:)` with an array sends one message to the channel of each `Outgoing` item in a single call, in chunks of at most 64 items.
It goes on past an item that fails, sets each item's `status`, and returns how many succeeded.
Every item's handles are consumed, even when the call throws.
A server answers all the requests it has read at once this way.

`read(_:)` with an array reads the next message from the channel of each `Incoming` item, in chunks of at most 64 items.
It goes on past a channel with nothing queued.
Each item gets its `message`, or a `status` saying why it has none (`shouldWait`, `peerClosed`), and the call returns how many messages were read.
Each message is first read into `readSlot` bytes (1024); a message that is larger stays queued and is read again, at its own size, in a second call.
A server reads every channel its port reported at once this way.

## Calls

`call` writes a message to `channel` and waits for the reply, until `deadline` (see sys-clock(2)).
The first four bytes of the request become the kernel's transaction id; the reply that echoes it comes to this caller alone.
It throws `timedOut` at the deadline.
The reply may be as large as a channel message can be.
It is `channel_call(2)`.

